  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nOver the next 24 hours an upper level shortwave will dive south \nthrough the northern Plains and phase with a trough already in \nplace across the central CONUS. A closed upper low will develop \natop the Ohio Valley region Thanksgiving day and into Friday,\nwith strong cyclogenesis taking place at the surface as well. \nThis system will bring impacts of various extents through the end \nof the week.\n\n\nPrecipitation: \n\nStarting with the most straight forward impact, precip. Relative to \n24 hours ago, rainfall amounts of trended downward and hi-resolution \nmodels are catching onto the typical rain shadow effect a little bit \nbetter. The official forecast largely ranges from 0.50 - 1.0 inches. \nThis aligns well with NBM probabilities, where prob of rain\ngreater than or equal to one-half inch is around 70 to 80%. Prob \nGTE 1.0 falls off sharply to the 20-40% range. Rainfall is \nexpected to build into the region Wednesday afternoon and evening,\ncontinue through much of Thanksgiving day, though it will be \ntrending lighter the later we get into the holiday. Some wrap \naround moisture on the backside of the system could promote a few \nflakes falling, particularly in the higher elevations, Thursday \nnight into Friday. Only light accumulations are expected at this \ntime. Temperatures would promote light rain or drizzle for any \nnorthern valley locations that see lingering light precip Friday.\n\nWinds: \n\nThis system is also expected to bring some gusty winds to the \nregion, though, this aspect of the forecast is a bit more complex. I \nwill start with the mountain winds as that is where the greatest \nimpacts are expected. Unfortunately there still remains some notable \ndifferences in peak intensity of wind gusts between the RRFS and \nHRRR. This is largely due to variations in the amplitude and \npositioning of the LLJ as it really starts. While RRFS peaks at \nabout 65kts with those greatest winds more to our east, the HRRR has \na more expansive area of 65kts H85 winds. Of course, this means \nthe HRRR has a higher frequency of wind gusts near 65mph across \nthe mountain zones while RRFS generally tops out at high end \nadvisory criteria. \n\nBecause the direction of flow is from the east as this LLJ ramps up \naround noon tomorrow through early Thanksgiving morning, made the \nfinal decision to go with a strongly worded advisory. The product \nfocuses on wind gusts up to 55mph but mentions most prone locations \nwith locally higher values near 60 to 65 mph within additional \ninformation. There could also be some gusts in the 30s to near \nadvisory criteria tonight due to a stronger pressure gradient near \nthe CAD (this was observed this morning at Cove Mountain), but will \nfocus the advisory on the period where the LLJ is amplified. \n\nValley locations are also expected to see some gusty winds as the \nsurface low deepens and strengthens the PGF by Thanksgiving \nafternoon into Friday. Broadly speaking, 20 to 30 mph gusts seem \nlikely, but some locations could gust near 35mph. At this time it \nlooks like valley locations will remain below advisory criteria for \nThursday and Friday. \n\nThis system will finally eject from the region with sunshine and dry \nweather returning Saturday. This will be short-lived, however, as\nan additional trough and cold front bring more rain chances late \nSunday/early Monday. Thursday and Friday will feature cooler \ntemperatures with the weekend returning closer to seasonal norms.",
